1
00:00:00,414 --> 00:00:03,689
Think about what you do before handing
off a pull request.

2
00:00:03,764 --> 00:00:07,777
You check the issue, summarize the diff,
and share the result of the tests.

3
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:12,280
If you ask for that same draft often,
you can give the assistant a skill with

4
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:13,755
this procedure.

5
00:00:13,805 --> 00:00:17,980
We’ll use a synthetic example and make
the output a draft that the human author

6
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:18,654
can review.

7
00:00:20,143 --> 00:00:24,168
In the training repository, open the
supported project skills location for

8
00:00:24,218 --> 00:00:25,493
Copilot.

9
00:00:25,568 --> 00:00:30,331
This example puts a folder named prepare
P R evidence under dot GitHub skills.

10
00:00:30,934 --> 00:00:33,459
Add a file named skill dot M D.

11
00:00:33,534 --> 00:00:36,934
Give it a matching name and a short
description that says which task it

12
00:00:36,984 --> 00:00:37,984
helps with.

13
00:00:38,059 --> 00:00:40,888
Both of those fields are useful when you
verify discovery.

14
00:00:42,372 --> 00:00:45,787
In the body, tell the skill to read the
supplied issue and diff,

15
00:00:45,837 --> 00:00:49,862
check the test output, and return a
draft with change and verification

16
00:00:49,912 --> 00:00:50,517
evidence.

17
00:00:51,130 --> 00:00:53,330
Include a step for missing items.

18
00:00:53,405 --> 00:00:58,330
If no test output is supplied, the draft
should say verification is missing.

19
00:00:58,405 --> 00:01:02,193
Keep this first skill focused on
preparing text for a human to review.

20
00:01:03,685 --> 00:01:07,410
Open a fresh Copilot chat and confirm
the skill is listed in the

21
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:09,310
customizations view.

22
00:01:09,385 --> 00:01:12,679
If your setup supports it, choose the
skill from the slash menu.

23
00:01:13,291 --> 00:01:17,266
Attach the synthetic issue, patch, and
passing test output.

24
00:01:17,341 --> 00:01:20,216
Now compare each draft claim with its
source.

25
00:01:20,291 --> 00:01:24,166
In this example, the issue is seventeen,
zero is rejected,

26
00:01:24,216 --> 00:01:26,653
and the supplied run shows four passing
tests.

27
00:01:28,143 --> 00:01:30,768
Try again with the test output missing.

28
00:01:30,843 --> 00:01:33,418
Read what the skill says about
verification.

29
00:01:33,468 --> 00:01:36,795
The expected result is a visible gap
that the author can fill.

30
00:01:37,433 --> 00:01:41,283
If it claims the tests passed based only
on the PR description,

31
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:43,558
revise the body and rerun.

32
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:47,283
Then try an unrelated request and see
whether the description makes the

33
00:01:47,358 --> 00:01:49,005
skill’s intended scope clear.

34
00:01:50,518 --> 00:01:53,943
Compare this skill with the repository
instruction file.

35
00:01:53,993 --> 00:01:57,943
The instruction file describes
conventions for work in this project.

36
00:01:57,993 --> 00:02:00,984
The skill carries a repeatable procedure
you can invoke.

37
00:02:01,586 --> 00:02:05,986
Tools let the agent perform actions such
as reading a supplied file.

38
00:02:06,036 --> 00:02:09,855
An approved M C P server can expose more
tools or resources.

39
00:02:10,444 --> 00:02:13,369
Our example works with supplied training
evidence,

40
00:02:13,419 --> 00:02:15,730
and the environment still controls
access.

41
00:02:17,181 --> 00:02:21,981
Pick another repeated task, like
summarizing a failed test for an author.

42
00:02:22,056 --> 00:02:25,879
Write a small skill with a clear
trigger, steps, and expected output.

43
00:02:26,472 --> 00:02:30,197
Try it with complete input and again
with one item missing.

44
00:02:30,272 --> 00:02:33,597
Ask a teammate to judge each result from
the evidence.

45
00:02:33,672 --> 00:02:37,072
If they can explain when the skill
applies and spot missing proof,

46
00:02:37,122 --> 00:02:38,922
you have a useful procedure to share.
